WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:23.838
Every first Saturday of the month, a church hall in Freiburg turns into a workshop. On long tables lie toasters, sewing machines, lamps and broken headphones. Between them sit people with screwdrivers, soldering irons and a great deal of patience. The idea of Repair Cafés comes from the Netherlands and has since spread to many countries.

00:00:24.088 --> 00:00:52.621
One of the helpers is Werner, 71, a former electrician. Today he is bent over an old vacuum cleaner that a young woman has brought in. 'Most appliances aren't really broken at all,' he explains. 'Often it's just a loose cable or a small blown fuse. But the manufacturers make it hard: many devices are glued instead of screwed together, and spare parts are hard to find.'

00:00:52.871 --> 00:01:16.686
The young woman is called Selin and studies environmental science. She could have bought a new vacuum cleaner for forty euros. 'But it's absurd to throw something away just because a part worth a few cents is broken,' she says. After half an hour the vacuum cleaner is humming again. Selin applauds; Werner smiles, satisfied.

00:01:16.936 --> 00:01:42.536
Politicians have now recognised the problem. The European Union has adopted a 'right to repair': manufacturers of certain appliances are to be obliged to supply spare parts and repair instructions for years. Werner doubts that it is enough. 'A law doesn't fix a toaster,' he says. 'For that you need people who know how.'

00:01:42.786 --> 00:02:11.557
For most visitors, though, money is not the main thing. Many come because the throwaway society annoys them. Others come mainly for the conversation and for the cake that volunteers bake every month. And some, says Werner, come simply to learn how their things work. 'Anyone who has once repaired something themselves shops differently afterwards.'
